# Releases and the Chrome Web Store

Every release of {= project_name =} reaches the Chrome Web Store by itself, signed in to Google without a stored secret. What only a person can do there is done once: the first upload, the listing and the privacy practices, and the sign-in. This page describes all of it, and what to do when a run of the workflow *Chrome Web Store* fails.

## How a release is made

Merging the pull request `chore: release x.y.z` of the release bot publishes the release. Its isolated build, `build-release.yml`, builds the zip of the store from the signed tag with `scripts/package-extension.sh`, attests its provenance and attaches it to the release with the SBOMs. Then the workflow *Chrome Web Store*, `.github/workflows/publish-store.yml`, delivers it:

- *the state of the store* signs in, asks the store for the state of the item and decides with `scripts/store-status.mjs`: a release that the store lacks goes to the store when no review is pending; while one is, the run waits, and a later day delivers it.
- *the package of the release* downloads the zip of the release, checks its provenance with `gh attestation verify` and its version, and keeps it for 30 days as an artifact of the run.
- *deliver to the Chrome Web Store* uploads exactly that zip with the Chrome Web Store API and submits it for review; Google publishes it after the review.

The workflow also runs every day, which delivers a release whose review had to wait, reports a review of more than 7 days, a rejection or a warning of Google, and keeps a refresh token in use. A prerelease, such as `1.3.0-beta.1`, stays off the store; its zip in the release has the version `1.3.0` in its manifest, as a manifest takes only numbers, and the whole version as `version_name`, for testers who load it by hand. Such a prerelease comes only from the beta channel (the variable `BETA_CHANNEL=true`), never from a line `Release-As: 1.3.0-beta.1`: the release bot would write that version into `extension/manifest.json`, which Chrome refuses, and the step *Extension* of the release PR says so.

## The package for the store

The extension is the folder `extension/`: its `manifest.json`, the languages in `_locales/`, and the scripts that `npm run build` compiles from `src/` into `extension/lib/`. `bash scripts/package-extension.sh` builds it and zips the contents of `extension/` without source maps, TypeScript sources and the configuration of Biome and TypeScript, with `manifest.json` at the root, as `dist/{= project_slug =}-<version>.zip`; the same commit makes the same zip, byte for byte. The release bot writes the version of each release into `package.json` and `extension/manifest.json`.

The step *Extension* of `scripts/check.sh` runs `node scripts/check-manifest.mjs`, which fails before the review of the store would:

- the manifest is Manifest V3, with the version of `package.json`, and without `key` or `update_url`;
- the name and the description fit the limits of the store, 75 and 132 characters, in every language of `extension/_locales/`;
- every permission, host and web-accessible resource of the manifest has its reason in `store/permissions.json`, and the file names nothing more: Chrome disables an installed extension after an update that asks for a new permission until its user accepts it;
- each language has its listing, `store/listing.<language>.md`, whose short description fits and whose *Permission justifications* name every permission and host.

The CI then loads `extension/` in Chrome for Testing, `node scripts/load-extension.mjs "$(bash scripts/chrome-for-testing.sh)"`, and fails when its service worker doesn't start.

## The first listing

The store makes an item only from a first upload by hand; every later version comes from the workflow.

1. Register as a developer in the [Developer Dashboard](https://chrome.google.com/webstore/devconsole), once, with a Google account that has 2-step verification.
2. Build the zip with `bash scripts/package-extension.sh`, and upload it with **New item**.
3. Fill in **Store listing** from `store/listing.en.md`, with the [images of the store](#the-images-of-the-store), and **Privacy practices**: the single purpose, a justification of each permission and host, the use of data and the three statements of the policies. A listing of another language is another `store/listing.<language>.md`; the dashboard takes it under the language of the listing.
4. Submit the first version for review.
5. Set the IDs of the item and of the publisher as variables of the repository: the item ID is in the address of the item in the dashboard, the publisher ID under **Account**:

    ```sh
    gh variable set CWS_EXTENSION_ID --repo {= repo =} --body ITEM_ID
    gh variable set CWS_PUBLISHER_ID --repo {= repo =} --body PUBLISHER_ID
    ```

6. Set up the [sign-in without a stored secret](#sign-in-without-a-stored-secret).

`python3 .github/blueprint.py checklist` shows both steps until their variables are set.

## The images of the store

The store shows these pictures, which the dashboard takes by hand; keep them in `store/images/`, made by a script, such as `scripts/screenshots.sh`, so that they follow the extension:

| Picture | Size | Needed |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Icon | 128 × 128 pixels, PNG, from the icons of the manifest | yes |
| Screenshots | 1280 × 800 or 640 × 400 pixels, one to five | at least one |
| Small promotional tile | 440 × 280 pixels | yes |
| Marquee promotional tile | 1400 × 560 pixels | no |

## The one-time set-up

The two jobs that talk to the store sign in one of two ways, and the variables decide which: once `CWS_WORKLOAD_IDENTITY_PROVIDER` and `CWS_SERVICE_ACCOUNT` are set, without a stored secret, else with the refresh token of the secrets of the environment `chrome-web-store`. The settings as code keep that environment to `main`.

### Sign in without a stored secret

A pool of Workload Identity Federation in a Google Cloud project takes the OIDC tokens of GitHub under a condition of its provider: the repository by its ID, which a new repository of the same name doesn't get, the branch `main` and the workflow `publish-store.yml`. No branch, fork or other workflow can trade a token, not even the Release workflow, whose jobs get OIDC tokens to sign the provenance. The pool may act as a service account that has no role in the project, and the Developer Dashboard admits that service account to the item.

1. Open the [Cloud Shell](https://console.cloud.google.com/home/dashboard?cloudshell=true) with the project of the dashboard selected, or create one for it, and run:

    ```sh
    project="$(gcloud config get-value project)"
    repo={= repo =}
    repo_id="$(curl -s "https://api.github.com/repos/$repo" | jq -r .id)"
    number="$(gcloud projects describe "$project" --format='value(projectNumber)')"
    gcloud services enable iamcredentials.googleapis.com sts.googleapis.com --project "$project"
    gcloud iam workload-identity-pools describe github --project "$project" --location global >/dev/null 2>&1 ||
      gcloud iam workload-identity-pools create github --project "$project" \
        --location global --display-name GitHub
    gcloud iam workload-identity-pools providers create-oidc {= provider =} --project "$project" \
      --location global --workload-identity-pool github --display-name "{= project_name | replace('"', '') | truncate(32, True, '', 0) =}" \
      --issuer-uri https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com \
      --attribute-mapping 'google.subject=assertion.sub,attribute.repository_id=assertion.repository_id' \
      --attribute-condition "assertion.repository_id == '$repo_id' && assertion.ref == 'refs/heads/main' && assertion.workflow_ref == '$repo/.github/workflows/publish-store.yml@refs/heads/main'"
    # One service account for every extension of the publisher, as the dashboard takes one.
    gcloud iam service-accounts describe "chrome-web-store@$project.iam.gserviceaccount.com" \
      --project "$project" >/dev/null 2>&1 ||
      gcloud iam service-accounts create chrome-web-store --project "$project" \
        --display-name "Chrome Web Store delivery"
    gcloud iam service-accounts add-iam-policy-binding "chrome-web-store@$project.iam.gserviceaccount.com" \
      --project "$project" --role roles/iam.workloadIdentityUser \
      --member "principalSet://iam.googleapis.com/projects/$number/locations/global/workloadIdentityPools/github/attribute.repository_id/$repo_id"
    echo "CWS_WORKLOAD_IDENTITY_PROVIDER=projects/$number/locations/global/workloadIdentityPools/github/providers/{= provider =}"
    echo "CWS_SERVICE_ACCOUNT=chrome-web-store@$project.iam.gserviceaccount.com"
    ```

2. In the [Developer Dashboard](https://chrome.google.com/webstore/devconsole), under **Account**, add the address of the service account, `chrome-web-store@PROJECT_ID.iam.gserviceaccount.com`. A publisher has one service account.
3. Set the two values that the last lines printed as variables of the repository, and start the workflow by hand, which checks the sign-in:

    ```sh
    gh variable set CWS_WORKLOAD_IDENTITY_PROVIDER --repo {= repo =} --body projects/NUMBER/locations/global/workloadIdentityPools/github/providers/{= provider =}
    gh variable set CWS_SERVICE_ACCOUNT --repo {= repo =} --body chrome-web-store@PROJECT_ID.iam.gserviceaccount.com
    gh workflow run publish-store.yml --repo {= repo =}
    ```

4. Once that run passes, delete the secrets of the refresh token, if there are any: they could still publish a version, and each run warns of them until they are gone. Delete the OAuth client under **APIs & Services → Credentials** of the project as well, which ends the refresh token at Google too:

    ```sh
    gh secret delete CWS_CLIENT_ID --env chrome-web-store --repo {= repo =}
    gh secret delete CWS_CLIENT_SECRET --env chrome-web-store --repo {= repo =}
    gh secret delete CWS_REFRESH_TOKEN --env chrome-web-store --repo {= repo =}
    ```

Nothing of this expires: the access token lasts 20 minutes, longer than either job may run, and each run gets a new one.

### Sign in with a refresh token

The way of before, for a Google account without a Cloud project of its own:

1. In a Google Cloud project, under **APIs & Services → OAuth consent screen**, create the consent screen as an external app with your own address as a test user, then set its publishing status to **In production**: while it stays in testing, Google expires every refresh token after seven days.
2. Under **Credentials**, create an **OAuth client ID** of the type **Desktop app**, and note its client ID and client secret.
3. Open this address in the browser, signed in as the developer, and allow the access; Google warns about an unverified app, which is your own:

    ```text
    https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/v2/auth?client_id=CLIENT_ID&redirect_uri=http://localhost:1&response_type=code&scope=https://www.googleapis.com/auth/chromewebstore&access_type=offline&prompt=consent
    ```

    The browser then lands on an address `http://localhost:1/?code=…` that nothing answers; copy the `code` from it and trade it within a few minutes, which answers with the `refresh_token`:

    ```sh
    curl https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token -d client_id=CLIENT_ID -d client_secret=CLIENT_SECRET -d code=CODE -d grant_type=authorization_code -d redirect_uri=http://localhost:1
    ```

4. Set the three secrets in the environment `chrome-web-store`:

    ```sh
    gh secret set CWS_CLIENT_ID --env chrome-web-store --repo {= repo =}
    gh secret set CWS_CLIENT_SECRET --env chrome-web-store --repo {= repo =}
    gh secret set CWS_REFRESH_TOKEN --env chrome-web-store --repo {= repo =}
    ```

The daily sign-in keeps the refresh token in use, so Google doesn't expire it after six months without use. It still ends with a new password of the Google account, when someone removes the access of the app under **Security → Third-party connections** of the account, or when the consent screen goes back to testing: then get a new one as in step 3 and set it as in step 4.

## When a run fails

A failed run names the reason and the fix in its summary and in an annotation, and the bot-health sweep of the night opens an issue with the label `bot-failure`, which the next run that passes closes:

- **A variable or a secret is missing:** set it as [the first listing](#the-first-listing) and [the sign-in](#sign-in-without-a-stored-secret) say.
- **Google turned the refresh token away** (`invalid_grant`), or **doesn't know the OAuth client:** [sign in without a stored secret](#sign-in-without-a-stored-secret), or set the secrets of [the refresh token](#sign-in-with-a-refresh-token) again.
- **Google's Security Token Service turned the OIDC token of GitHub away:** the provider was deleted or disabled, or its condition doesn't admit the run, such as one started by hand on another branch than `main`, or after the repository or the workflow got a new name or owner. **IAM & Admin → Workload Identity Federation** of the project shows the condition, which step 1 of [the sign-in](#sign-in-without-a-stored-secret) sets.
- **Google issued no access token of the service account:** the pool lacks the role *Workload Identity User* on the service account, or the project the IAM Service Account Credentials API; step 1 of [the sign-in](#sign-in-without-a-stored-secret) grants and enables both.
- **The store didn't tell the status of the item:** with 401 or 403, the Developer Dashboard doesn't name the service account under **Account** (step 2 of [the sign-in](#sign-in-without-a-stored-secret)); with 404, `CWS_EXTENSION_ID` or `CWS_PUBLISHER_ID` names no item of the publisher.
- **The release has no zip of the extension:** a release made before the repository became a Chrome extension of the blueprint; release a new version.
- **The store refused the upload:** the run names the reason of the store. A version that isn't above the one in the store needs a release above it; a refused manifest needs the fix that the store names, which `node scripts/check-manifest.mjs` checks, and a new release; while a review is pending, the item takes no new package, and the daily run delivers it after the review, or cancel the review in the Developer Dashboard.
- **The store refused to submit the release** (`INVALID_ITEM_METADATA`): the store took the package but the Developer Dashboard lacks something this version needs, which the API can't set. Most often it is a justification under **Privacy practices** for a permission or a host that is new in the version, or the listing of a new language. The run lists what the version asks for and which languages it speaks. Paste the justifications from `store/permissions.json` and the listings from `store/listing.<language>.md`, then save the draft, which names any other field that is missing. Then submit the version in the dashboard, or start the workflow by hand with its tag: `gh workflow run publish-store.yml --repo {= repo =} -f tag=vX.Y.Z`.
- **Rejected:** the mail of Google and the dashboard say why. Fix the cause and release a new version; when only the listing or the privacy practices had to change, change them in the dashboard and start the workflow by hand with the tag.
- **Warned or taken down:** the dashboard says which policy and until when. Fix it there or with a new release; for a taken-down item, start the workflow by hand with the tag of the fixing release.
- **In review for more than 7 days:** look in the dashboard whether Google asks for anything, or contact the support of the Chrome Web Store. A new permission makes a review take longer.
- **A higher version in the store:** a version went to the store by hand without its release; release a version above it.

A run that failed for a reason of GitHub or of the network needs nothing: the next daily run delivers the release.

## The network of the jobs

The jobs that sign in to the store could publish a version to every user, so each job reaches only the hosts it needs. [Harden-Runner](https://github.com/step-security/harden-runner) blocks every other one, each job first waits until it lets those hosts through, and the jobs run without sudo and containers:

| Job | Secrets | Hosts |
| --- | --- | --- |
| *the state of the store* | none, or those of the refresh token | GitHub, `oauth2.googleapis.com`, `sts.googleapis.com`, `iamcredentials.googleapis.com`, `chromewebstore.googleapis.com` |
| *the package of the release* | none | GitHub and Sigstore, for the release and its provenance |
| *deliver to the Chrome Web Store* | none, or those of the refresh token | `oauth2.googleapis.com`, `sts.googleapis.com`, `iamcredentials.googleapis.com`, `chromewebstore.googleapis.com` |

Only the two jobs that sign in may ask GitHub for an OIDC token. The job that uploads runs no code of the repository and no npm package: it takes the zip of the job before it, which checked the provenance of the release.
